llainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-02T00:27:54-08:00","id":1527752,"title":"","content":"Development details matter when the product itself doesn't give an answer.<br /> <br /> And Cult of Fenris doesn't give an answer why THEY are the bad tribe. Or it does, but it goes straight back to the development.<br /> <br /> The whole point of Cult of Fenris being the antagonist tribe is due to all of them suffering from Hauglosk. You gain Hauglosk from breaking Chronicle Tenets (which doesn't have a good in-universe explanation but I digress.) The problem arises when you consider what these invisible Chronicle Tenets must be because neither Red Talons poisoning entire villages nor BSDs seem to have broken any. I am pretty sure if I joined a W5 game with a Red Talon that intentionally spreads CoVid, I'd be Hauglosking in no time. BSDs by their existence alone should break many tenets. Yet, Red Talons start with 0 Hauglosk and the BSD write-up doesn't mention them suffering from it.<br /> <br /> The only thing that the Talons and BSDs lack is the very on-the-nose Nordicness of the CoF. Which then directly leads us to the development of W5 where the Brand Manager told a person working on a positive version of the tribe that the very idea of them being Nordic was the issue.<br /> <br /> I am not trying to woobify Nordic people. But given the material we are given, this is the best conclusion I can come to.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-02T01:33:07-08:00","id":1527754,"title":"","content":"And it isn't like the other official &quot;explanation&quot; is very helpful either. I don't think the explanation of the developers being unable to come up with four verbs that are distinct from the Ahroun Auspice was ever very convincing. It always came off as rather bizarre rather than plausible. So neither the book nor the developers ever really made a case for why W5 needs the &quot;Fenrir have fallen to Hauglosk&quot;-bit... except for what we learned from JF Sambrano about how Karim Muammar looks at the Get of Fenris (and how uninterested he was in hearing other opinions on that).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27535-knightingale","name":"Knightingale","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27535_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1492"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-02T01:56:50-08:00","id":1527757,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Psicose</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1524711-review-of-w5?p=1527739#post1527739\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm confused, how much of the old lore I should take into consideration, or should I take into consideration, I don't even know anymore.</div> </div> </div> </div>Like most things in W5 the answer is &quot; No one Knows.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/479-damian-may","name":"Damian May","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar479_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-02T02:21:52-08:00","id":1527758,"title":"","content":"It isn't just CoF that showcases how disjointed W5 is, wolf-born are unwanted yet involved. The way the Litany is presented is odd, because the text says no one uses the laws as laws, yet we are shown some of the laws are things like keeping werewolves secret and not eating humans. If they wanted to give the STs true freedom, they could have just left the Litany up to the ST so they could decide how oppressive it truly is.<br /> <br /> Mind, I have zero issues with the game involving punching fascists. That is very much part of fighting the Wyrm.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-02T02:41:43-08:00","id":1527761,"title":"","content":"There are also times in the book where authoritarianism and fundamentalism are described as a general issue of Garou-society. So if Fenrir are more extreme than Garou in general, where else would this go other than Fenrir being turned into Nazis by W5? And if you don't go that far, you have the issue of there being little to distinguish between the negative extremes the book assigns to Garou in general and the supposed extremes exhibited by the Fenrir. Because that's another thing: The W5 corebook already has a low opinion of Garou-society in general, so if the Cult of Fenris is supposed to be even more extreme than that... That's how you get that section on Fascism at the end of the book where Hauglosk, Fenrir, ecofascism and Nazis are mentioned in connection to each other.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27535-knightingale","name":"Knightingale","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27535_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1492"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-02T04:42:04-08:00","id":1527764,"title":"","content":"So, the first sentence should explain why I'm responding to something most people can't see. CT messaged me the text, but there's some formatting loss. This is going to be a bit longer since I'm going to leave the full post up as well as I can, and then repeat passages to do my normal specific responses after without any context being lost.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sorry, I accidentally edited my post and who knows how long it'll be until it's approved by a mod.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Saur Ops Specialist</b>: &quot;You're still going to say that even after we've had that look backstage about what was going on? It's a purely meta move based on shit meta.&quot;<br /> <br /> I mean to say it's utterly irrelevant. The background doesn't matter for the world building save for the people involved in the production. For the world building, only what's on the text is important.<br /> <br /> <b>Heavy Arms</b>\u200b: &quot;You don't get to demand everyone else only analyze things according to the Death of the Author or other deconstructionist methods<br /> <br /> If you want to apply Death of the Author, that's on you. If you want the rest of us to, you need to convince us why it's important to do so. Demanding it just means your arguments don't matter.&quot;<br /> <br /> Saying, &quot;I don't care what you think if you don't agree with my demanding that out of universe material be incorporating in analyzing in-universe portrayals&quot; is not the winning argument you think it is. You're welcome to say you don't want to discuss the issue with me but I am stating my position that I think discourse benefits from examining the material presented versus what's going on behind the scenes.<br /> <br /> <b>Heavy Arms</b>\u200b:\u200b &quot;This is lying by abusing the &quot;game of telephone.&quot; The head person in charge of the game wanted to make the Cult of Fenris explicitly Nazis based on the Swords of Heimdall from previous edition. He had to be talked out of it, over an extended period of time. Trying to paint it as just &quot;someone&quot; is some hardcore revisionism.&quot;<br /> <br /> And there you have it there. He was talked out of it. Which is what exactly is the point I'm bringing up. There are no Swords of Heimdall, there is no Nazis, there is no death of Little Brother. The Get of Fenris as presented are villains based around their own fanaticism and devotion to the cult of Gaia.<br /> <br /> <b>Heavy Arms</b>\u200b:\u200b &quot;Please name one fascist movement that wasn't also racist. Please explain how comparing something to Nazism isn't further implying a fundamental racist character to the Cult of Fenris?&quot;<br /> <br /> The Get of Fenris.<br /> <br /> Because they're fictional werewolves. They're black, white, and every other shade of the rainbow looking down on other Garou as well as human beings as a whole. They're every bit as racist as the Friends of Humanity against the X-men or the Galactic Empire against aliens.<br /> <br /> Which is to say, &quot;Against people who are not real because they are not real.&quot;<br /> <br /> Given the way that Alt-Righters try to co-opt fiction, this is a smart move.<br /> <br /> <b>Heavy Arms</b>\u200b:\u200b &quot;No, it's not, because you're using lore from the old games. W5 says the old game lore doesn't count anymore. There was no Get of Fenris in W5 that was like the Get of Fenris from previous editions. The only lore that counts in W5 lore if you want to say the Cult of Fenris is justified being the way it is.&quot;<br /> <br /> The old lore is absolutely not canon, except for all the stuff that is from the previous games that is alluded to. They could say none of it matters and it's a reboot but they haven't and they make frequent references to it. However, the old lore absolutely was building up to one of the Tribes falling.<br /> <br /> You know why I know that? Because it's literally one of the Apocalypse scenarios.<br /> <br /> <b>Heavy Arms</b>\u200b:\u200b &quot;Dude, we get it You need time to come around to WoD5e's fuck ups. You're going to find something you think is good, and use it to defend way more than you should, for years, until you finally can't any more. It's a pattern we're all very familiar with. But that's also draining on everyone else's patience. CT's &quot;V5 is not just the greatest VtM edition, but greatest game ever&quot; take 100, but the Cult of Fenris this time, is not endearing, enlightening, or insightful.<br /> <br /> It's just painful to watch you act out the same loop over and over instead of just realizing it's time to skip the ending that you get to every time anyway.&quot;<br /> <br /> Actually, you can just say, &quot;CTP really loved Chicago by Night 5E and its books. He hated The Sabbat and Second Inquisition which he DID give too much slack.&quot;<br /> <br /> Anyone who claims that CBN and Cult of the Blood Gods aren't among the greatest books ever made for V:TM as a whole is wrong, though. They also are probably doing so because they want to pretend everything V5 is terrible.\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>-------------------------------<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For the world building, only what's on the text is important. </div> </div> </div>Why? You've asserted this multiple times You've been challenged to explain it. Instead you're just repeating that only Death of the Author counts.<br /> <br /> It's facially ludicrous to treat the world building of a game based on allegory and political satire as something where the context of its writing is irrelevant. If we were talking about a game where there is no specific allegorical moral, or satirical criticism, and any reading of that is extrapolation from the text that might just be reading into the author's subconscious bias? At best we have a great example of why a deconstructionist analytical method is useful. But even then it isn't exhaustive. It's value is what happens when you stack it up against context-conscious analysis and see if the conclusions concur or diverge, where, and then discuss why. Simply insisting on Death of the Author analysis only is not promoting discussion of the text, it's just a way to claim objective &quot;truth&quot; for your opinions and thus reject any disagreement.<br /> <br /> Allow me to ask you a hypothetical example here. And please do not skip this if you actually care about having this discussion.<br /> <br /> If it came out that there were leaked exchanges from employees at an TTRPG company that they only included an anti-fascist disclaimer because legal forced them to do so, but they purposefully built the world so it's easy to play fascists because they want to promote fascism even if they had to do it subversively, would you insist those statements are irrelevant to analyzing the world building in question? Would you be so sure in your dismissals that just because it doesn't contradict the lore in some fashion, that's the only thing that matters? Would you be comfortable posting in public, &quot;sure it's world building designed to make you want to be a fascist, but it's still good lore accurate world building, so it's a good thing to have,&quot; and then defend that?<br /> <br /> Because there's no middle ground here. Either you're the strict deconstructionist you're insisting we all be here, or you're not. There is no, &quot;well it can't be good world building if it's pro-fascist&quot; weaseling out of this either. W5's world building makes the fascists sympathetic, and the non-fascists unsympathetic. If it didn't use out-of-universe statements to clarify it's intent in the text that this is to be subversive against fascism, it would be indefensibly a pro-fascists game: the fascist Cult of Fenris is, however flawed, fighting to save the world when times are the bleakest, while the Garou that weren't dedicated enough to become extremists in the name of ecofascism are sitting around twiddling their thumbs as the Age of the Apocalypse slowly kills the world around them. Without further context (which is in the book, but not in the world building part), that's an allegory for totalitarian ideologies because they're the only ideologies that actually care about fighting the immediate fight so we all survive to bicker about other things later. You are praising this exact world building as it is. You're relying on a statement of intent that made it into the book to justify it, while decrying people that then want to include statements of intent that didn't make it in; even though both are statements of authorial intent, and not world building<br /> <br /> .<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Saying, &quot;I don't care what you think if you don't agree with my demanding that out of universe material be incorporating in analyzing in-universe portrayals&quot; is not the winning argument you think it is. </div> </div> </div>I never said &quot;I don't care what you think.&quot; And you of all people should know exactly how I feel about it when people put words in my mouth like this.<br /> <br /> Here's why what I actually said the winning argument I think it is:<br /> <br /> 1) Your demands for in-universe material only is not established as either a best practice or an objective truth to world building analysis. You have not made the case for why in-universe only should be the approach we all agree to. Your insistence that we do despite obvious push back against it means you're losing this specific portion of the debate because you're not backing up your assertions with argumentation; just more numerous assertions.<br /> <br /> 2) You're not even keeping to in-universe only material. You're relying on authorial intent statements from the book, and you're relying on out-of-universe (even if from a related universe) material as well. &quot;The bigotry is fictional&quot; is also not an in-universe only argument; it's the exact opposite of an in-universe argument and the characters aren't Deadpool. If you can't make your point solely on in-universe to W5 content, then you've obviously lost the argument that the rest of us should.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You're welcome to say you don't want to discuss the issue with me but I am stating my position that I think discourse benefits from examining the material presented versus what's going on behind the scenes. </div> </div> </div>If this is your intent, you're doing a piss poor job of it. If you want to present an actual deconstructionist textual analysis, present it to us, and make the case for what it adds to the discourse? Please. Actually do that. For real. Show us that. Because right now you're not showing the benefits of that school of literary criticism, you're just dismissing arguments that come from any other.<br /> <br /> And just to be clear: you don't seem to have noticed that nobody thinks that the idea of the Get of Fenris, as they were in the legacy editions, falling into the unbridled extremism introduced in W5 is nonsense. The Get of Fenris fell and became bad guys is not a plot development that anyone has opposed as an in-character development on the grounds of the character of the Tribe pre-W5. The opposition is within the context of the rest of W5's world building (or lack there of), or out-of-universe issues that &quot;it makes sense based on the legacy Get at the end of Revised&quot; is not a meaningful counterpoint to. You have not addressed why the rise of the Cult of Fenris is logical and fine, within the same world building that has the Red Talons and Black Spiral Dancers in the current states they are present as. You're not addressing world building like how Tribes are no longer primarily cultural constructs transmitted by social means: W5 Garou become a member of a Tribe because their &quot;soul&quot; resonates most with the Patron of the Tribe, and thus they are members of the Tribe. The social constructs of the Tribe come after that. That's why it's so important to address the world building aspects of Bat and Griffon. It's no longer &quot;which evil bastard faction finds you first and inducts you,&quot; like it was in the old editions. In W5, the setting is, &quot;it's which Patron you resonate with the most.&quot; How all three coexist when vying for freshly changed Garou with hearts ripe for lives of violent brutal extremism is a logical issue to the coherence of the setting of W5.<br /> <br /> And another point: there are no rules for playing a non-Cult of Fenris, but still Get of Fenris character. There are rules for playing a character who's inner nature resonated with the Tribe before they became the Cult, but stopped once they did, and the new most resonant Patron took them in. Just like there's no extant White Howlers that didn't become Black Spiral Dancers in W5 (yet anyway), there's no Get of Fenris that didn't join the Cult. The setting turned it into a binary state of falling with the Tribe, or getting spiritually slotted somewhere else.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They're every bit as racist as the Friends of Humanity against the X-men or the Galactic Empire against aliens. </div> </div> </div>\u200b<br /> Then you haven't named a non-racist fascist movement. You've just named fictional fascists with fictional targets for their bigotry. They're still racists. It doesn't matter if they're not real, or the targets of their racism aren't real. Fascism is inherently a racist ideology. You claimed &quot;racism&quot; isn't in the book. It is, because fascism and Nazism are in the book, and you can't have those as subjects and not also be talking about racism. This is how allegory and satire work: you discuss a topic in a fictional context to make critical points about the real world.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The old lore is absolutely not canon, except for all the stuff that is from the previous games that is alluded to. </div> </div> </div>Where does W5 say that anything &quot;alluded&quot; to is fully part of W5? There's lots of things in W5 that allude to things in the older books that don't make any sense to extrapolate as the older material on that topic transferring in full. The Silent Striders are alluded to have been cursed in the ancient past, but we're most certainly not supposed to try the old material on that curse as canon in W5 (and even if we were, it would make zeros sense).<br /> <br /> Again: either follow your own demands for strict in-universe only material, or don't. Pulling stuff from the old books is not pulling from the W5 in-universe material. Even if its alluded too.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You know why I know that? Because it's literally one of the Apocalypse scenarios. </div> </div> </div>Yeah, the part where it talks about what it would look like for each of the Tribes to fall. They were all setup to be flawed enough where that was a possibility. That doesn't explain why we have the Cult of Fenris instead of the Cult of Griffon.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Actually, you can just say, &quot;CTP really loved Chicago by Night 5E and its books. He hated The Sabbat and Second Inquisition which he DID give too much slack.&quot; </div> </div> </div>Just saying that would be pointless, because it wouldn't impress on you that you're doing the exact same thing all over again. You're cherry-picking things you love about WoD5e material (and you've done this going back to the pre-release V5 playtests), and hanging defending the rest of the content on those high points well past what you have since acknowledged you should have. You're doing it again. The Cult of Fenris is not CbN5. It's V5: Sabbat. Eventually you're going to come around to this, because you do come around to these things, but it would be a lot more pleasant to talk about these things if you would see this pattern and break it already.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Anyone who claims that CBN and Cult of the Blood Gods aren't among the greatest books ever made for V:TM as a whole is wrong, though. They also are probably doing so because they want to pretend everything V5 is terrible. </div> </div> </div>Do you really think this is a defense of how you're going about discussing the Cult of Fenris?<br /> <br /> Also:<br /> <br /> I'll never put any city book in any of my greatest VtM books, because I find the entire concept of city books to be a waste of time. It's true of every WoD and CofD city book ever printed. The best ones get a &quot;meh&quot; from me because I can recognize quality, but to me it's still squandered on a pointless type of supplement. Is that about V5 being terrible?<br /> <br /> CotBG? Honorable mention at best. High quality sure, but inseparable from V5, which isn't completely terrible, but I have V20 and VtR 2e to do anything I would do with VtM over V5. CbN5, however much I hate the format of such books, is still cool if you like city books and want some inspiration for Chicago for an urban fantasy game. You could recommend it to someone that wants a city book for VtR that isn't WoD: Chicago with all of it's crossover stuff. You could recommend it as a setting for d20 Modern, or a Forged in the Dark vampire game that's not set in VtM specifically. CotBG doesn't have that going for it. It's not particularly worth it for a V20 game, let alone VtR or even farther afield.<br /> <br /> I know you love them, but it's a preference, not a fact. You're just reinforcing the perception that you're digging in to die on the hill of the cherries you picked, regardless of what you say in the process.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-02T04:45:24-08:00","id":1527765,"title":"","content":"Well, that's kinda funny. CT got hit with the edit glitch, messaged me a post so I could post it and reply... and my post didn't get approved on the first go... the system must really not want us to keep trying....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-02T05:25:14-08:00","id":1527769,"title":"","content":"Well hopefully it can be done but I submit to the power of the Philodox.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i